{{- /*
Other MCP clients. Cursor, source: https://cursor.com/docs/mcp (2026-11):
{"mcpServers": {name: {"url", "headers"}}} in ~/.cursor/mcp.json (user) or
.cursor/mcp.json (project), no "type" for a remote server, "${env:NAME}"
substitutes a variable in url and headers, dynamic registration as a public
client; the desktop application signs in with redirect
http://localhost:8787/callback (its web agents' https redirect is not
admitted). VS Code, source:
https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agents/reference/mcp-configuration
(2026-11): the VS Code format {"servers": {name: {"type": "http", "url",
"headers"}}} in .vscode/mcp.json or the user profile's mcp.json (its
guided flow labels both destinations deprecated but still reads them), the
portable format {"mcpServers": {...}} in ~/.copilot/mcp-config.json; it
registers a loopback and an https redirect URI, of which the console keeps
the loopback one. The docs link is VS Code's reference, which covers both
formats (the MCP site's remote-servers guide is about Claude's connectors).
*/ -}}
{{define "name"}}VS Code, Cursor and other clients{{end}}
{{define "docs"}}https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agents/reference/mcp-configuration{{end}}
{{define "add"}}{{end}}
{{define "login"}}{{end}}
{{define "check.note"}}Open the client's list of MCP servers: {{.Server}} shows as connected once you have signed in.{{end}}
{{define "pack.document"}}agents{{end}}
{{define "pack.project"}}AGENTS.md{{end}}
{{define "user.path"}}~/.cursor/mcp.json{{end}}
{{define "user.format"}}json{{end}}
{{define "user"}}{
  "mcpServers": {
    {{q .Server}}: {
      "url": {{q .URL}}
    }
  }
}
{{end}}
{{define "project.path"}}.vscode/mcp.json{{end}}
{{define "project.format"}}json{{end}}
{{define "project"}}{
  "servers": {
    {{q .Server}}: {
      "type": "http",
      "url": {{q .URL}}
    }
  }
}
{{end}}
{{define "static.path"}}~/.cursor/mcp.json{{end}}
{{define "static.format"}}json{{end}}
{{define "static"}}{
  "mcpServers": {
    {{q .Server}}: {
      "url": {{q .URL}},
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": {{q (print "Bearer ${env:" .TokenEnv "}")}}
      }
    }
  }
}
{{end}}
{{define "static.env"}}export {{.TokenEnv}}="$({{.TokenCommand}})"{{end}}
{{define "static.note"}}The client sends the variable's value as the bearer token. Use it where token sign-in is your break-glass path; the sign-in above is the normal one.{{end}}
